  "result": "Neither: \"more agents always give better results\" is false, so five versus ten is the wrong question. Start with one agent, and add another only when you can name what it fixes.\n\nYou probably got there by assuming that splitting work is like adding people to a team. Each agent, though, is another model loop with its own context, and it costs tokens every time it runs. Agents also pass information to each other as text, and details get lost or garbled in those handoffs. For a small budget, that is a real loss.\n\nYour assistant has three jobs: answer from manuals, check the calendar and book time. One agent with three well-described tools can do all of them. A second agent would pay off only if one job needs a very different context. An example is manuals so long that searching them would crowd out the booking logic.\n\nCan you name one thing in your assistant that a single agent could not handle?",
